{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>A test that passes 98% of the time isn't passing</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You write a test for a component that shows a "Saved!" toast 2 seconds after a save button is clicked, then hides it. The test clicks save, waits, and asserts the toast appears. It passes on your laptop every time. In CI, it fails maybe once every 40 runs, always on the same line.
    </p>
    <p>
      The test isn't testing your component's logic — it's racing a real 2-second clock against however busy the CI runner happens to be that day.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Any test that does <code>await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2000))</code> (or an equivalent real wait) and then asserts is coupling test correctness to wall-clock scheduling. Under CI load — shared runners, other jobs, throttled CPU — a "2 second" wait can take 2.3 seconds, and a test written to check state at exactly the 2-second mark starts flaking.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Fake timers replace <code>setTimeout</code>/<code>setInterval</code>/<code>Date</code> with an in-process, synchronous clock the test controls directly — advancing it doesn't wait for real time to pass, it fires the callbacks scheduled for that point instantly. The assertion becomes deterministic because there's no real clock left to race against.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>The test controls the clock instead of waiting on it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of your test actually pausing for 2 real seconds, a fake-timer library swaps out <code>setTimeout</code> with its own version. When your test calls "advance time by 2000ms," every timer scheduled to fire within that window runs immediately, in order — no real waiting happens at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Vitest's <code>vi.useFakeTimers()</code> and Jest's <code>jest.useFakeTimers()</code> both patch the global timer functions for the duration of a test. <code>vi.advanceTimersByTime(2000)</code> (or Jest's <code>jest.advanceTimersByTime</code>) synchronously fires anything due in that window; <code>vi.runAllTimers()</code> fires everything currently scheduled, including timers that schedule further timers.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This only fixes flakiness caused by <em>timer</em> races. A test can still flake for unrelated reasons — real network calls, unflushed microtasks, DOM layout timing — so fake timers are the fix specifically when the thing you're waiting on is a <code>setTimeout</code>/<code>setInterval</code> your own code scheduled, not a general-purpose flake cure.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The debounced search box that failed one run in forty</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's search input debounced its API call by 300ms — type, wait 300ms of silence, then fire. Their test typed a query, used a real <code>await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 350))</code> to "wait past the debounce," then asserted the API mock was called once.
    </p>
    <p>
      It failed intermittently in CI, never locally. Nobody could reproduce it on demand, which made it worse than a test that just failed outright — it looked like a real product bug at first.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Switching to <code>vi.useFakeTimers()</code> plus <code>vi.advanceTimersByTime(300)</code> removed the real wait entirely. The test now asserts the mock was called zero times immediately after typing, then exactly once after advancing the fake clock past the debounce window — both checks are synchronous and can't be affected by how loaded the CI runner is.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team also caught a latent bug this way: advancing to exactly 300ms (not 350ms) revealed the debounce comparison used <code>&gt;</code> instead of <code>&gt;=</code>, so the call fired one tick later than the component's own advertised delay. The real-timer version had enough slack (350ms padding) to hide this; the fake-timer version, being exact, didn't.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

