{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>What fake timers don't fix, and cleaning up after them</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Always call <code>vi.useRealTimers()</code> (or Jest's equivalent) after a test that used fake timers, usually in an <code>afterEach</code>. Forgetting this leaks the fake clock into the next test file's timers, which can cause a completely different, much more confusing kind of flakiness.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Fake timers don't fake <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> or real network I/O by default — libraries differ, so check whether your test needs <code>vi.useFakeTimers({ toFake: [...] })</code>-style scoping if a component also depends on rAF timing, or you'll get a different flake, not a fix.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Testing Library's <code>waitFor</code> polls with real microtask/macrotask ticks under the hood; mixing it with fake timers requires either <code>vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync</code> or wrapping assertions carefully, because <code>waitFor</code>'s own internal polling can itself stall waiting on a timer that will now never fire on its own. Check your fake-timer library's docs for the specific async-flush helper it ships for exactly this interaction.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

